## 5. Quick checklist

| ✅ | What you need |
|---|---|
| **Layer type** | `type: "color-relief"` |
| **Source** | A `raster-dem` source (same one you’d use for `setTerrain`) |
| **Ramp expression** | `["interpolate", ["linear"], ["elevation"], …]` inside `color-relief-color` |
| **Opacity** (optional) | `color-relief-opacity` (0-1) |
| **Resampling** (optional, GL JS 5.20+) | `resampling: "linear"` or `"nearest"` |
| **No pre-rendered tiles** | All coloring happens client-side, no extra raster tiles required |
| **Works with or without 3-D terrain** | `color-relief` is independent of `map.setTerrain()` |

---

### TL;DR

*Add a `color-relief` layer, point it at your `raster-dem` source, and set `color-relief-color` to an `interpolate` expression that uses `["elevation"]`. The expression defines the hypsometric colour ramp (blue → green → brown → white).*  

That’s all you need to get a client-side hypsometric tint on a DEM in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!

**Short answer:** Yes.  
MapLibre GL JS does not generate contours itself, but the **`maplibre-contour`** library can turn any `raster-dem` source into a virtual vector source that streams contour tiles on-the-fly (inside a Web-Worker). You then add a normal `line` (or `symbol`) layer that reads the generated vector tiles.

Below is a complete, step-by-step guide that you can copy-paste into a fresh MapLibre project.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the helper library

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm – whichever you use
npm i maplibre-contour   # or: yarn add maplibre-contour
```

> **Why a library?**  
> The DEM tiles are encoded (Terrarium, Mapbox, etc.) and need to be decoded, interpolated, and contoured. `maplibre-contour` does all that in a background worker, then serves the result through a custom URL protocol (`contour://…`). No server-side preprocessing is required.

---

## 2️⃣ Create the DEM source (the same one you would use for hillshade/terrain)

```js
// The DEM tiles you already have – they must be a raster-dem source
const demTileUrl = 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'; // any format MapLibre can read
const demEncoding = 'terrarium'; // or 'mapbox', 'raw', etc. – must match the source tiles
```

---

## 3️⃣ Spin up a `DemSource` from `maplibre-contour`

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';

// 1️⃣ Create the contour helper
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // URL template for the DEM tiles
  url: demTileUrl,

  // How the DEM values are encoded in the raster tiles.
  // Common values:
  //   'terrarium' – 16-bit signed integer packed in RGB (MapLibre default)
  //   'mapbox'    – 16-bit unsigned integer packed in RGB
  //   'raw'       – 8-bit signed integer (rare)
  encoding: demEncoding,

  // The highest zoom level that the DEM tiles exist for.
  // The contour generator will never request tiles above this.
  maxzoom: 12,

  // Whether to run the contouring in a Web-Worker (highly recommended)
  worker: true
});

// 2️⃣ Register the helper with the MapLibre instance.
//    This adds the custom `contour://` protocol handler.
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

> **Tip:** If you already have `map.setTerrain({source: 'my-dem', ...})` you can reuse the same DEM URL/encoding – just create a second `DemSource` for the contours.

---

## 4️⃣ Add a *virtual* vector source that points to the contour protocol

```js
// The protocol URL is built by the helper. It contains all the
// parameters the worker needs to generate the right contour tiles.
const contourUrl = demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
  // ── How often you want contours (in metres) at each zoom level.
  //    The object maps zoom → [minorInterval, majorInterval].
  //    Minor contours are drawn thinner; major ones thicker / labelled.
  thresholds: {
    // zoom : [minor, major] (both in metres)
    11: [200, 1000],
    12: [100, 500],
    13: [50, 250],
    14: [25, 100],
    15: [10, 50]
  },

  // The property name that will hold the elevation value in each feature.
  // You can pick any name you like; the default is "ele".
  elevationKey: 'ele',

  // The property that marks a feature as a "major" contour.
  // The default is "level". 1 = major, 0 = minor.
  levelKey: 'level',

  // Optional: give the source a stable name (used for caching).
  // If omitted a random UUID is generated.
  sourceId: 'contours'
});

// Register the source in the style
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is a *single* tile URL – the worker will
  // replace {z}/{x}/{y} internally.
  tiles: [contourUrl],
  // The worker can generate tiles up to this zoom (usually a few
  // levels higher than the DEM source so contours stay crisp).
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

> **What’s happening under the hood?**  
> When MapLibre asks for a tile like `contours/0/0/0`, the `contour://` handler intercepts the request, pulls the needed DEM tiles (e.g. `z=11,12,13`), runs a marching-squares algorithm in the worker, and returns a **vector tile** that contains line features with the properties you defined (`ele`, `level`).

---

## 5️⃣ Draw the contour lines

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  // The name of the source-layer inside the generated vector tiles.
  // `maplibre-contour` always uses the same name: "contours".
  'source-layer': 'contours',

  // Styling – you can make major contours thicker / brighter.
  paint: {
    // Dark semi-transparent lines for both minor & major
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',

    // Vary width based on the "level" property:
    //   level = 1 → major contour → 1.5px
    //   level = 0 → minor contour → 0.8px
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, 1.5,   // major
      0, 0.8,   // minor
      0.5       // fallback
    ]
  }
});
```

### Optional: label the major contours

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1], // only major lines
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']], // elevation value
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-offset': [0, 0.5],
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

---

## 6️⃣ (Optional) Combine with other elevation layers

You can have **all four** elevation visualisations from the same DEM source:

| Layer type | Purpose | Example |
|------------|---------|---------|
| `hillshade` | Classic black-white relief | see skill “Hillshade” |
| `color-relief` | Hypsometric tint (elevation → colour) | see skill “Color-relief” |
| `contour` (above) | Vector contour lines generated at runtime | this guide |
| `terrain` (`map.setTerrain`) | 3-D mesh you can pitch/rotate | see skill “3D terrain” |

Just make sure the DEM source (`type: 'raster-dem'`) is added **once** and referenced by each of those layers.

---

## 7️⃣ Performance & practical tips

| Issue | Why it matters | How to mitigate |
|-------|----------------|-----------------|
| **Worker overhead** | The first few tiles need to be decoded & contoured, which can take ~100 ms on a mid-range phone. | Keep `worker: true`. The worker is reused for all tiles; subsequent tiles are fast. |
| **Too many zoom levels** | Generating contours at very high zoom (e.g. 18) can flood the worker with many tiny DEM tiles. | Limit `maxzoom` on the contour source to ~15 (or whatever your app needs). |
| **Too fine a threshold** | Very small intervals (e.g. 1 m) produce thousands of line segments per tile → UI lag. | Choose intervals that make sense for the map scale; use the `thresholds` object to coarsen at low zooms and fine-tune at high zooms. |
| **Mismatched DEM encoding** | If you set `encoding: 'terrarium'` but the tiles are Mapbox-encoded, elevations will be wildly wrong. | Verify the DEM provider’s documentation; test a single tile with `map.queryTerrainElevation([lng, lat])` before adding contours. |
| **Caching** | Contour tiles are generated on-the-fly each session. | The library caches generated tiles in memory; you can also enable `worker: true` + `maxCacheSize` (if you need a custom cache). |

