Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for turning the empty “page-background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are native MapLibre GL JS – no extra plugins are required.

---

## 1️⃣  Prerequisites – you already have these

```js
// 1️⃣  Create the map
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // any style you like
  center: [13.4, 52.5],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 70,               // you already set this
  bearing: 0,
  antialias: true          // important for smooth terrain shading
});

// 2️⃣  Add the DEM source (raster-dem) and enable terrain
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',   // ← your DEM tileset
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 12,
  encoding: 'terrarium'   // or 'mapbox' – must match your DEM
});

map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });
```

If you can already see the 3-D relief, the code above is working.

---

## 2️⃣  Add a **sky** object

The sky is **not a layer** – it lives at the root of the style (or can be added later with `map.setSky`).  
All sky-related properties are listed in the style spec under **sky**.

```js
// 2️⃣  Add a sky (you can call this any time after the map is ready)
map.on('load', () => {
  map.setSky({
    // overall colour of the dome (day-time blue)
    'sky-color': '#87CEEB',

    // colour of the horizon line – usually a lighter version of sky-color
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',

    // how quickly the sky colour blends into the horizon (0-1)
    // 0 = hard line, 1 = very smooth transition
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

    // optional: add a faint atmospheric glow at the very edge
    // (only works when you also set a horizon-fog blend)
    'atmosphere-blend': 0.2
  });
});
```

### What each property does

| Property | Typical range | Effect |
|----------|---------------|--------|
| `sky-color` | any CSS colour | Base colour of the dome. |
| `horizon-color` | any CSS colour | Colour exactly at the horizon line. |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | 0 – 1 | Controls how soft the transition is. 0 = sharp line, 1 = very gradual. |
| `atmosphere-blend` | 0 – 1 | Adds a faint “glow” at the very top of the sky (optional). |

> **Tip:** If you want a sunset-type sky, just change `sky-color` and `horizon-color` to warm oranges / pinks and increase `sky-horizon-blend` for a smooth gradient.

---

## 3️⃣  Add **fog** (the haze that meets the ground)

Fog only appears when **terrain is enabled** – exactly the situation you have.  
Fog is also a root-level object, set with `map.setFog` (MapLibre v2.4+).  
If you are on an older version that only has `setSky`, you can still add fog via the `sky` object – the spec treats fog as part of the sky.

```js
map.setFog({
  // colour of the fog – usually a desaturated version of the sky colour
  'color': '#c0d8ff',

  // how far from the camera the fog starts (in metres). 0 = right at the camera.
  // A small value gives a thin haze; a larger value makes the fog appear farther away.
  'range': [0.5, 5]   // [near, far] in kilometres (MapLibre interprets as km)
});
```

### Fog properties explained

| Property | Typical values | Effect |
|----------|----------------|--------|
| `color` | any CSS colour | Tint of the haze. |
| `range` | `[near, far]` in **kilometres** | Controls where the fog begins and ends. Smaller `far` → thicker fog close to the viewer. |
| `high-color` (optional) | CSS colour | If you want the fog to become a different colour at higher altitudes (e.g., turning whitish near the peaks). |
| `high-range` (optional) | `[near, far]` | Where the `high-color` starts to blend in. |

> **Why fog works only with terrain:** The fog shader needs the 3-D mesh to know how far each fragment is from the camera. If `setTerrain` is off, the map is flat and the fog is ignored.

---

## 4️⃣  Full minimal example (copy-paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Sky + Fog + Terrain</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [13.4, 52.5],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // ---- DEM source & terrain -------------------------------------------------
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 12,
      encoding: 'terrarium'   // <-- match your DEM
    });
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // ---- SKY ------------------------------------------------------------------
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#87CEEB',
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'atmosphere-blend': 0.2
    });

    // ---- FOG ------------------------------------------------------------------
    map.setFog({
      'color': '#c0d8ff',
      'range': [0.5, 5]   // near 0.5 km, far 5 km
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser (or paste the code into a CodePen/JSFiddle) and you’ll see:

* A blue-to-white sky that follows the horizon.  
* A gentle, semi-transparent haze that starts a few hundred metres away and thickens toward the horizon, giving the classic “ground-meets-sky” look.  
* Your 3-D terrain still visible because `setTerrain` is on.

