## 5️⃣  Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Sky stays black / empty** | `map.setSky` called **before** the map is ready, or you are using an old MapLibre version (< 2.4) that doesn’t have `setSky`. | Call `map.setSky` inside a `map.on('load', …)` handler, and upgrade to the latest MapLibre GL JS. |
| **Fog never appears** | Terrain is not enabled, or you are using `map.setFog` on a version that only supports fog via the `sky` object. | Ensure `map.setTerrain` is called **before** `map.setFog`. If you’re on a version < 2.4, add fog properties to the `sky` object instead: `map.setSky({ ..., 'fog-color': '#c0d8ff', 'fog-ground-blend': 0.5 })`. |
| **Horizon line is a hard edge** | `sky-horizon-blend` is left at the default `0`. | Increase it to `0.3-0.7` for a smoother transition. |
| **Fog looks too thick / too thin** | `range` values are not tuned for your zoom level. | Adjust the two numbers: a smaller `far` value makes fog denser; a larger `near` value pushes the start farther away. |
| **Background colour still shows through** | You have a `background` layer with an opaque colour that sits **above** the sky (layer order matters). | Move the `background` layer to the **bottom** of the style (or set its `paint['background-color']` to `rgba(0,0,0,0)` if you want the sky to be visible). |

---

## 6️⃣  Optional – Adding a sun / moon (visual cue only)

MapLibre does **not** support the Mapbox-only `sky-atmosphere-sun` properties, but you can fake a sun with a **symbol** layer placed at a high altitude:

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'sun',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: {
      type: 'FeatureCollection',
      features: [{
        type: 'Feature',
        geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [13.4, 52.5] },
        properties: { icon: 'sun' }
      }]
    }
  },
  layout: {
    'icon-image': 'sun',          // you need to add a sun image to the sprite
    'icon-size': 2,
    'icon-allow-overlap': true
  }
});
```

Place the point at a high elevation (e.g., 10 km) using the `elevation` property in a **color-relief** or **fill-extrusion** layer if you need it to interact with terrain. This is purely decorative – it does not affect the sky shading.

---

## 7️⃣  TL;DR – The minimal commands you need

```js
// after map is loaded and terrain is on:
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#87CEEB',
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5
});

map.setFog({
  'color': '#c0d8ff',
  'range': [0.5, 5]   // km
});
```

That’s it! Your map will now show a proper sky dome with a soft haze that meets the terrain, eliminating the flat page background you were seeing above the horizon. Enjoy the view!

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** recipe for putting a pre-rendered shaded-relief JPEG tile set under all of your vector data (including the label layer) in a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1.  What you need to know

| What you have | How MapLibre treats it |
|---------------|------------------------|
| **JPEG raster tiles** (e.g. `https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg`) | A **`raster` source** → a **`raster` layer**. No DEM, no hillshade, no `color-relief`. |
| **Vector data + labels** | Usually added as `fill`, `line`, `symbol`, … layers. They sit **above** any raster layer that appears earlier in the style’s layer stack. |
| **Desired order** | 1️⃣ raster-relief → 2️⃣ all your vector layers (including the label `symbol` layer) |

The only thing you have to do is:

1. **Declare a raster source** that points at your JPEG tiles.
2. **Add a raster layer** that uses that source.
3. **Make sure the raster layer is placed before the first vector layer** (or explicitly move it with `map.moveLayer`).

That’s it – no hillshade, no DEM, no extra paint properties.

---

## 2.  Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Shaded-relief basemap under vector data</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style you like)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},          // we’ll add sources later
      layers: []            // we’ll add layers later
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  When the map is ready, add the raster source + layer
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // ---- 2a.  Raster source (JPEG tiles) ----
    map.addSource('relief-basemap', {
      type: 'raster',
      // Replace the URL template with yours
      tiles: [
        'https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
      ],
      tileSize: 256,          // most JPEG/PNG tiles are 256 px
      // optional: if your tiles are in a different projection (e.g. 3857) you can set `scheme: 'xyz'`
      // optional: set a maxzoom/minzoom if you know them
    });

    // ---- 2b.  Raster layer that draws the source ----
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'relief-basemap-layer',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'relief-basemap',
      // Paint properties are optional – you can tweak opacity, fade, etc.
      paint: {
        // make the relief a little translucent if you want the vector base map to peek through
        // 'raster-opacity': 0.95,
        // control how quickly tiles fade when you zoom/pan
        // 'raster-fade-duration': 300
      }
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add your vector data (or keep existing ones)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Example: a vector source with a line layer
    map.addSource('roads', {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'https://mytileserver.com/roads/tileset.json'   // <-- your vector source
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'roads-line',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'roads',
      'source-layer': 'roads',   // name of the layer inside the vector tileset
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#ff6600',
        'line-width': 2
      }
    });

    // Example: a label (symbol) layer that should sit on top
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'city-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'roads',
      'source-layer': 'places',
      layout: {
        'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
        'text-size': 14,
        'text-anchor': 'top'
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#000',
        'text-halo-color': '#fff',
        'text-halo-width': 2
      }
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  (Optional) Ensure ordering – move raster below everything else
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // If you added vector layers *before* the raster layer, just move it:
    // map.moveLayer('relief-basemap-layer', map.getStyle().layers[0].id);
    // The call above moves the raster layer to the very bottom of the stack.
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Add source** (`type: 'raster'`) | Tells MapLibre where to fetch the JPEG tiles. |
| **Add layer** (`type: 'raster'`) | Renders those tiles. The layer’s `id` can be anything you like (`relief-basemap-layer`). |
| **Paint options** (optional) | `raster-opacity`, `raster-fade-duration`, `raster-brightness-min/max`, etc., let you fine-tune the look. |
| **Add vector layers** | Anything you add *after* the raster layer will automatically be drawn on top. |
| **Re-ordering** (`map.moveLayer`) | If you ever need to change the order later, `moveLayer` lets you push the raster to the bottom (or any other position). |

---

## 3.  Common “gotchas” & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **The raster tiles appear *above* my labels** | You added the raster layer *after* the label layer, or you inserted it before but later called `addLayer` for the label with `insertBefore` omitted. | Either add the raster **first**, or explicitly move it to the bottom with `map.moveLayer('relief-basemap-layer', map.getStyle().layers[0].id)`. |
| **Tiles look too bright/dark** | JPEG tiles are often rendered for a specific background (e.g., a dark basemap). | Use `paint: { 'raster-brightness-min': 0.5, 'raster-brightness-max': 0.8 }` or adjust `raster-contrast`. |
| **Tiles flicker when zooming** | The default `raster-fade-duration` is 300 ms. | Set `'raster-fade-duration': 0` for instant tile swaps. |
| **Only part of the map shows the raster** | The source’s `minzoom`/`maxzoom` or the map’s current zoom is outside that range. | Verify the source’s `minzoom`/`maxzoom` (or omit them) and make sure your map’s zoom is within the tile set’s coverage. |
| **CORS errors** | Your tile server does not send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers. | Enable CORS on the tile server, or proxy the tiles through a server you control. |
| **Tiles are blurry at high DPI** | The server only provides 256 px tiles, but you’re on a device with device-pixel-ratio > 1. | Add `tileSize: 256,` (already there) and set `maplibregl.Map({ pixelRatio: window.devicePixelRatio })` – MapLibre will request higher-resolution tiles automatically if they exist (`@2x` naming convention). |

---

## 4.  Quick checklist before you ship

1. **URL template** – Replace `https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg` with the exact pattern your server expects.
2. **CORS** – Verify the tiles load in the browser console (no 403/404/CORS errors).
3. **Layer order** – Open the style inspector (`map.getStyle().layers`) and confirm the raster layer is the *first* entry (or at least before any `symbol`/`line`/`fill` layers you want on top).
4. **Performance** – If the raster tiles are large (e.g., 512 px) or you have many vector layers, test on a low-end device. You can lower `raster-fade-duration` and/or set `raster-opacity` < 1 to let the GPU blend more efficiently.
5. **Responsive design** – If you support retina displays, make sure the tile server can serve `@2x` tiles (e.g., `relief@2x/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg`). MapLibre will request them automatically when `pixelRatio > 1`.

---

## 5.  TL;DR – One-liner for the core part

```js
// 1️⃣ Add raster source
map.addSource('relief', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
  tileSize: 256
});

// 2️⃣ Add raster layer (make sure it’s added *before* any vector layers)
map.addLayer({
  id: 'relief-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'relief'
});
```

Everything else (vector sources, label layers, ordering) follows the normal MapLibre workflow.

---

### 🎉 That’s it! 🎉

Your beautiful pre-rendered shaded-relief JPEG tiles are now the **bottommost basemap**, and all of your vector data—including labels—will render cleanly on top. If you run into any specific error messages or need help tweaking opacity/contrast, just drop the details and I’ll help you fine-tune it. Happy mapping!
